WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:02.447
PRERNA BELLARA: Look,
unicorns do exist.

00:00:02.447 --> 00:00:03.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Psh.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.200
We all know unicorns
are mythical creatures,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.720
whereas dinosaurs actually
did graze the very land we're

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.610
standing on.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.300
PRERNA BELLARA: OK, well,
before we go back and forth

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.665
about our own existence, the
real question of the decade

00:00:12.665 --> 00:00:14.980
is, do you know where your
sensitive data exists?

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.305
Or is it a myth that your
PII and sensitive information

00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:20.080
are going back and forth,
and your company will never

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.040
be leaked?

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.030
STEPHANIE WONG: It
sounds like we can prove

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:23.270
that that's actually a myth.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.520
PRERNA BELLARA: Indeed, it is.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.200
But Google's Data
Loss Prevention API

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.520
can help classify and redact
information at the source.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.502
Enough of us talking.

00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:31.460
Let's jump into a demo.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.862
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.120
So this is a great opportunity
for talking about the Data Loss

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.120
Prevention API.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.970
And it's a need of
the hour, right?

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:45.880
In a way.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.400
Because if you
think about it, it's

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.710
reported that 2.5
quintillion bytes of data

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.400
are generated every day.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.080
That's a lot of information, and
a lot of sensitive information

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.850
that is generated by users
and millennials like you

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.994
and I. In fact, I
was told that 90%

00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:01.410
of the data that
we're using today

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.800
was generated just in
the last few years.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.920
Now, where does
DLP come into play?

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.440
Whenever we're talking
to our company in HR,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.360
or whenever we're talking
to our credit card

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.550
company or our
credit score company,

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.740
we tend to exchange some
sensitive information,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.090
such as our phone numbers,
our social security numbers,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.590
ZIP code, date of
birth, et cetera.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.150
DLP helps you classify
and redact information.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.220
How does it do that?

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.210
DLP API will traverse
through your raw data,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.711
classify it into a social
security number, phone number,

00:01:31.711 --> 00:01:32.210
and--

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.760
there are about 40 or 50
types of classifications

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.670
like this that you can
see on the website.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.570
It redacts that
information, and then

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.880
lets you use it for
further analysis,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.280
secure sharing
within your company,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.390
and for app development.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.041
Let's look at an app
development section.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:50.290
There's a situation that
we've all seen before, right?

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.760
There's a customer
agent, and there's

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.670
Alice, who's a customer,
who's trying to get an Anvil

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.110
application to work correctly.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.610
Neil has asked her
for the last four

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.630
digits of her social security
number and contact information.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.070
Alice has clearly overshared.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.620
She's not only given her
entire social security number,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.699
she's given her email
address and a photo

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.740
of her social security card.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.770
Now, enterprises want to
store this information

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.020
for further data
analysis and improvement.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.290
But if they store
this, they're exposing

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.644
Alice's private information
to the entire organization.

00:02:21.644 --> 00:02:23.060
And they can
potentially get sued.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.850
Alice can potentially
lose her social security

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.510
and her identity.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.310
How do we fix that?

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.890
As soon as we click
End Chat, our DLP API

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.410
runs in the background,
traverses through this chat,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.000
and redacts the information.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.760
You can see that the phone
number has been masked.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.440
Her social security
has been masked.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.200
Only the last four digits
have been maintained.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.140
Her email address is
completely blocked out.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.090
And this is one of
my favorite features.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.580
The social security number,
despite it being a photo,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.020
the numbers have been masked.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.290
And it's classifying it as a
social security number, as well

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.620
as the likelihood that this is
a very likely chance of being

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.510
a social security number.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.080
Like that, you have others,
like US Census names.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.630
And all of this
information can now

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:08.530
be stored very easily
into their database.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.860
Now, let's look at what it
looks like behind the scene.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.720
There's a Google
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.350
Yes, it's in the cloud, which
has all your raw data, right?

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.040
We're looking at an
examples right now,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.184
which has customer data,
HR data, and a mix of data.

00:03:21.184 --> 00:03:23.100
There's what the data
looks like in real life.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.790
There's a customer ID,
their first name, last name,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:27.990
and some comments.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:29.940
These are just garbage
comments, but you

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.070
can see that there are some
numbers over there that

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.800
resemble a social
security number,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.890
or somebody's phone number,
or a credit card number.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.347
Let's see how DLP
handles this information,

00:03:40.347 --> 00:03:42.180
because when we're
storing this information,

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.929
we don't want to store
their email address

00:03:43.929 --> 00:03:46.890
or their social security number.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.830
All of this information,
after it is run through DLP,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.900
we store it into BigQuery.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.180
This is what the data
looks like after DLP

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.100
has redacted the information.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.190
You can see it gives you the
table name, the row number,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.380
it tells you that it
was in column comments,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.555
and this was the value.

00:04:02.555 --> 00:04:04.680
And it was an email address,
and it was very likely

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.370
that it was an email address.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.910
So now you know the row which
has sensitive information

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:09.947
and the value.

00:04:09.947 --> 00:04:12.030
So the next time you want
to move this information

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.620
from one place to
another, you will

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.780
be cognizant to remove
the value or to be

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.120
wary of sharing this
information, or at least

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.790
this row.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.850
This was another query that
our team came up with just

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.150
to show the number of
occurrences of each attribute

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.160
type and the percentage.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.140
This, again, is in BigQuery.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.240
You can see it's a
three-line query that's

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.830
just coming up
with a small metric

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.970
that we have come
up with ourselves.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.261
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow, I don't
know what's more magical--

00:04:38.261 --> 00:04:39.290
you, or the DLP API.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:40.150
PRERNA BELLARA: But
don't be fooled.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.420
The DLP API is not a myth.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.920
It's an API, like
the name suggests,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.960
that prevents unauthorized
users from sending

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.614
sensitive or unauthorized data.

00:04:46.614 --> 00:04:48.530
STEPHANIE WONG: Can't
get more real than that.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:49.720
Thanks again, Prerna.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.594
PRERNA BELLARA: Sure.

00:04:50.594 --> 00:04:52.620
Please comment, like,
and subscribe below.

